From 34e93dec077d09dc3d8514c383fd8949c7ee0278 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Players Engineer <omar@blockcast.network>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 21:04:50 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] registry: concurrent bounded tag lookup and untag (backport
 a2afe23f38)

Backport of distribution/distribution a2afe23f386e827d1975530aab12010f0be2a774
("add concurrency limits for tag lookup and untag", Liang Zheng) onto
goharbor/distribution ac7c4cedc2f4d45347630beceaaa1639bb9c771a, the
revision Harbor v2.15.4 ships as registry_DO_NOT_USE_GC.

tagStore.Lookup listed every tag in the repository and read each
current/link serially. DeleteManifest calls it on every manifest delete,
so Harbor GC cost per manifest grew linearly with the repository's tag
count times the storage backend's per-object round trip.

- tagStore.Lookup reads tag links through an errgroup bounded by the
  tag store's concurrencyLimit; the first non-PathNotFound error cancels
  the remaining reads and fails the lookup (no partial result).
- DeleteManifest untags the referencing tags through an errgroup bounded
  by storage.DefaultConcurrencyLimit.
- New RegistryOption TagLookupConcurrencyLimit and config key
  storage.tag.concurrencylimit (must be a non-negative int; NewApp
  panics otherwise, exactly as upstream). Unset or 0 means
  runtime.GOMAXPROCS(0).
- Uses golang.org/x/sync v0.22.0, already recorded in this revision's
  go.sum, through its Go module rather than the retired vendor.conf.

Deliberate deviations from upstream, both so that a manifest DELETE
never reports success while tags still point at the deleted manifest:

1. Cancelled request. Upstream shadows ctx with the errgroup's context
   and stops launching link reads once it is done; g.Wait() then returns
   nil and Lookup returns the tags read so far with no error. The request
   context is cancelled on client disconnect or caller timeout, so
   DeleteManifest (which has already deleted the manifest) untags only a
   partial set, leaving the other tags pointing at the deleted manifest.
   Here Lookup keeps the caller's context and, after g.Wait(), returns its
   error if it is done -- never a partial set, for any caller. And
   DeleteManifest runs the lookup and untag that follow a successful
   manifest delete on context.WithoutCancel(request context): failing the
   request instead would leave every tag dangling while nobody sees the
   error (the client has gone; Harbor GC retries the DELETE, gets 404 and
   counts it as success). Finishing the cleanup is what the serial code
   did on S3 in practice, because the s3-aws driver ignores the request
   context.
2. Failed untag. Upstream DeleteManifest assigns the collected Untag
   errors to imh.Errors and then writes 202 Accepted anyway. Here, if any
   Untag fails, the handler returns the errors without writing 202.

Upstream's example config files set concurrencylimit: 8, and its
docs/configuration.md shows 8 in the sample config and the tag example.
That 8 has no derivation: the example config files are left unchanged
and the ported docs show a placeholder instead, so the documented
default stays GOMAXPROCS.

Tests: upstream configuration test ported; new storage tests compare the
concurrent Lookup with the verbatim serial algorithm over many tags with
missing links, prove the read concurrency reaches and never exceeds the
limit, check error propagation, and check that a context cancelled
before or during the lookup fails it; new handler tests cover the config
validation, multi-tag untag on DELETE, the fail-closed 202 on a failing
untag, and a request cancelled during the tag lookup still untagging
every referencing tag.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
